  ],
  "text": "Ignore relativity and your maps app drifts about ten kilometres off within a day. A satellite does not know where you are. It only says who it is and what time it is. Your phone compares that time to its own and works out how long the signal took. That gives a distance. One distance puts you on a sphere. Two puts you on a circle. Four satellites pins you to one point, and fixes your phone's own sloppy clock as well. Which means everything depends on the clocks agreeing. And they do not. Up there gravity is weaker, so the satellite clock runs faster than yours. It is also moving fast, which makes it run slower. The two pull opposite ways. Gravity wins, by about thirty eight microseconds a day, and light crosses a lot of ground in that. So the correction is built in before launch. Relativity is not abstract. It is in your pocket. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
